From 42a872a0f52f89e7f22d225c4c36717889f89ab3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 03:46:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add the B-150 workpad

Hub record for the ontology-ci pre-push hook fix. Kept as a separate commit
from the patch itself, because dfo-salmon-ontology is a shared repository:
if the pull request is approved, the patch commit is the one that belongs in
it and this one can be dropped.

Refs hub queue item B-150.
---
 .hub/workpads/B-150.md | 239 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 239 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 .hub/workpads/B-150.md

diff --git a/.hub/workpads/B-150.md b/.hub/workpads/B-150.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3bb64a3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.hub/workpads/B-150.md
@@ -0,0 +1,239 @@
+# B-150 — The gcdfo pre-push hook cannot fail, and discards the artifact `make ci` just rebuilt
+
+Claim `a-8e439a1f641ee603`, session `fleet-2026-09-16-B-150`, branch
+`agent/B-150/a-8e439a1f641ee603`.
+
+**Nothing was pushed.** `dfo-salmon-ontology` is a shared member repository, so
+under `HUB.md` § *Hand back* the hand-back ends before the push. Confirmed three
+ways before any work started: the participation table under `HUB.md` § *The
+standing authorization* lists `dfo-salmon-ontology` as "two, and it is another
+organization's — grant applies: **no**"; `queue/config.yaml` carries `solo:
+false` for it; and `./scripts/hub claim B-150` printed the same finding
+unprompted. `HUB.md` § *Which pull requests need Brett* item 4 says the same
+thing from the other side.
+
+## What changed, and where
+
+One file: `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, the `ontology-ci` hook. 39 insertions, 7
+deletions — most of it a comment block; the behavioural change is the tail of
+one `entry` line.
+
+Before, the `entry` chain ended:
+
+```
+... else make ci; fi; git checkout -- docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json || true
+```
+
+After:
+
+```
+... else make ci; fi; ci_status=$?; if [ "$ci_status" -ne 0 ]; then git checkout -- docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json; fi; exit "$ci_status"
+```
+
+That one edit closes both halves of the defect.
+
+**(a) It could not fail.** A shell returns the status of the last command in the
+list. The last command was `git checkout -- … || true`, which is 0 whether the
+checkout succeeds or fails, so the hook exited 0 for every build including
+failing ones. `ci_status` now captures the build's status immediately and the
+chain ends `exit "$ci_status"`.
+
+**(b) It discarded a build product.** `make ci` regenerates
+`docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` — the path is inside `CI_ARTIFACT_PATHS` in
+the `Makefile` — and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` rejects *any* uncommitted change
+left after `make ci` across the whole worktree. The hook's `files` pattern
+restricts it to changes to `ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl`, which is precisely when
+that artifact is expected to change, so the unconditional checkout threw away
+locally exactly what CI then demanded. The restore now runs only on the failure
+path.
+
+**The failure-path restore is kept on purpose, and that is what makes half (b)
+safe without waiting for B-0.** `docs-widoco` rsyncs raw WIDOCO output over
+`docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` before the normalizer runs, so a build that
+dies in between leaves raw generator bytes in the tree that a later run adopts
+as its normalizer baseline and goes green over. That is B-0, and
+`docs/tech-debt.md`'s 2026-08-16 entry ("A failed `make docs-widoco` can
+re-baseline WebVOWL output to raw generator bytes") prescribes exactly this
+restore as the local remedy — **"before retrying a failed docs build"**, not
+after every push. Scoping the checkout to the failure path therefore preserves
+the whole of its remaining rationale while removing the harm, so B-150's
+sequencing note is satisfied by the second of its two options: recorded as safe
+without B-0 rather than deferred behind it.
+
+## Commands and results
+
+`make ci` runs here in ~77s with ROBOT 1.9.8 and WIDOCO 1.4.25 in `tools/`
+(gitignored) and `SMN_FLAT_TTL` pointed at the sibling `salmon-domain-ontology`
+checkout. The deliberately broken ontology is a `gcdfo:` class with no
+`gcdfo:theme`, which `scripts/sparql/theme-coverage.rq` reports as "missing
+theme" and which makes `make ci` exit 2. It is a throwaway fixture, reverted
+after each run; it is not in the diff, and the worktree carries no ontology
+change.
+
+### Failing before
+
+Deliberately broken ontology. `make ci` fails:
+
+```
+$ make ci
+make ci EXIT=2
+❌ Theme coverage found issues. Inspect release/tmp/theme-coverage.tsv.
+```
+
+The hook, against that same ontology:
+
+```
+$ pre-commit run ontology-ci --hook-stage pre-push --files ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+Run full ontology CI bundle (make ci)....................................Passed
+>>> pre-commit EXIT=0
+```
+
+**A build that exits 2 produced a hook that says `Passed` and exits 0.** The
+push would have gone through.
+
+The discarded artifact, separately. Working tree holding only a label edit to
+`ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl`, `docs/` at HEAD. `make ci` alone regenerates four
+artifacts:
+
+```
+$ make ci && git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- .
+ M docs/gcdfo.jsonld
+ M docs/gcdfo.owl
+ M docs/gcdfo.ttl
+ M docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json
+ M ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+```
+
+The hook, from the same starting state:
+
+```
+$ pre-commit run ontology-ci --hook-stage pre-push --files ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+$ git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- .
+ M docs/gcdfo.jsonld
+ M docs/gcdfo.owl
+ M docs/gcdfo.ttl
+ M ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+```
+
+`docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` is in `make ci`'s output and gone after the
+hook, while its three siblings survive.
+
+### Passing after
+
+Same deliberately broken ontology, same command:
+
+```
+Run full ontology CI bundle (make ci)....................................Failed
+- hook id: ontology-ci
+- exit code: 2
+❌ Theme coverage found issues. Inspect release/tmp/theme-coverage.tsv.
+>>> pre-commit EXIT=1
+```
+
+Same label edit, same command — the artifact survives:
+
+```
+$ git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- .
+ M .pre-commit-config.yaml
+ M docs/gcdfo.jsonld
+ M docs/gcdfo.owl
+ M docs/gcdfo.ttl
+ M docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json
+ M ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+```
+
+and the tree is now one CI would accept, because the full artifact set stages:
+
+```
+$ make ci-sync-artifacts
+✅ Staged generated docs artifacts after make ci:
+   docs/gcdfo.jsonld
+   docs/gcdfo.owl
+   docs/gcdfo.ttl
+   docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json
+```
+
+Clean, valid, unchanged tree — the hook must not fail, and must not touch
+anything:
+
+```
+Run full ontology CI bundle (make ci)....................................Passed
+>>> pre-commit EXIT=0
+$ git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- .
+ M .pre-commit-config.yaml
+```
+
+Failure path still restores, and still fails. `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json`
+dirtied by hand to stand in for raw WIDOCO bytes, plus the broken ontology:
+
+```
+before:  M docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json
+         M ontology/dfo-salmon.ttl
+
+Run full ontology CI bundle (make ci)....................................Failed
+- hook id: ontology-ci
+- exit code: 2
+>>> pre-commit EXIT=1
+
+after:   (ontology.json restored — no status line)
+```
+
+Both properties hold at once: the poisoned baseline is cleaned up **and** the
+failure is not masked.
+
+### One observation about the harness, worth recording
+
+`pre-commit` exits 1 on a *successful* build whenever the hook modified files
+("files were modified by this hook"). That is the harness's own check, not the
+hook's exit status, and it is correct behaviour: it means an ontology change is
+being pushed whose regenerated artifacts are not committed, which is what CI's
+drift gate would reject. It masked nothing before the fix and masks nothing
+after, but it is why reading a `pre-commit` exit code alone cannot tell you
+whether the gate works — the two reproductions above separate the build's status
+from the harness's by using a failure that never reaches the docs step.
+
+## What I did not do, and why
+
+- **No permanent regression test.** The repository has no test harness — no
+  `scripts/tests`, no pytest or bats wiring in the `Makefile` or
+  `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. Adding one plus its CI wiring is a larger change
+  to a shared repository than B-150's `retires_when` asks for, which requires a
+  demonstration ("a deliberately broken ontology must turn the push red") rather
+  than a standing check. Filed below as a candidate.
+- **No change to `docs/tech-debt.md`.** Its 2026-08-16 entry is B-0's to retire,
+  and B-0's `retires_when` says so explicitly, including that the entry needs
+  more than deletion because its stated *Intended fix* is the git-HEAD baseline
+  Brett's 2026-09-14 ruling rejected. Editing it here would collide with that.
+- **No ontology change.** The broken-ontology fixture was reverted; the diff is
+  one file. No term IRI is chosen, changed or removed by this patch, so
+  `AGENTS.md`'s term-selection rule has nothing to report against it. The
+  fixture minted a throwaway IRI to make the validator fail and is not in the
+  diff.
+
+## Found, belonging elsewhere
+
+- **B-0** — unchanged and still open. This patch deliberately preserves the
+  behaviour B-0 will make redundant, and the hook comment names B-0's tech-debt
+  entry as its retirement condition.
+- **Candidate, no item.** `scripts/pre-commit-validate-ontology.sh` is
+  unreferenced anywhere in the repository — not by `.pre-commit-config.yaml`,
+  the `Makefile`, the CI workflow, or any document outside the immutable
+  `docs/releases/` snapshots. Its own header says it "runs the same validation
+  as the CI workflow", so it is a dead second implementation of the gate this
+  item just repaired, and a reader who finds it may reasonably believe it is
+  what runs. The repository's own `AGENTS.md` names "one clear active path" as
+  a rule. Evidence: `grep -rn "pre-commit-validate-ontology" .
+  --exclude-dir=.git` returns nothing but the file itself.
+
+## Retirement conditions of what this change adds
+
+The patch adds one conditional branch, and it carries its own condition in the
+hook's comment block:
+
+> **Retires when:** the 2026-08-16 `docs/tech-debt.md` entry retires. Its fix
+> makes `docs-widoco` restore the pre-run bytes itself on failure, at which
+> point the failure branch has nothing left to do and it, and the note, are
+> deleted rather than left as a second restorer.
+
+That is B-0. Nothing else here is a guard, suppression, skip or allowlist entry;
+the rest of the change removes a suppression rather than adding one.
